Patches applied to Fish Speech S2-Pro at /opt/prism42/infra/b300/services/fish-speech/
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Goal: drop `/v1/tts` HTTP TTFB (curl time_starttransfer, also first WAV-header
byte-over-the-wire) on a streaming request. Originally measured at ~9.86 s
(WAV header arrived only after full semantic generation).

Result after patch: WAV-header-over-the-wire TTFB ~0.003 s (3,000x faster).
Total audio-byte arrival is still bottlenecked by the engine's monolithic
generate_long pass — see post-mortem. But any downstream client that opens
an audio stream (LiveKit, ffmpeg -re, aplay -f wav) can now see the WAV
header + content-type immediately and set up its playback buffer.

PATCH A — src/tools/server/api_utils.py
---------------------------------------
--- a/src/tools/server/api_utils.py
+++ b/src/tools/server/api_utils.py
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+import asyncio
+import threading
 from argparse import ArgumentParser
 from http import HTTPStatus
 from typing import Annotated, Any
@@ -70,10 +72,36 @@


 async def inference_async(req: ServeTTSRequest, engine: TTSInferenceEngine):
-    for chunk in inference(req, engine):
-        print("Got chunk")
-        if isinstance(chunk, bytes):
-            yield chunk
+    """Bridge the sync inference generator to async so ASGI can flush each chunk
+    (especially the WAV header) the instant it is produced, rather than blocking
+    the event loop while LLAMA generates the entire utterance."""
+    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()
+    q: asyncio.Queue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=64)
+    _SENTINEL = object()
+
+    def _producer():
+        try:
+            for chunk in inference(req, engine):
+                if isinstance(chunk, bytes):
+                    # Thread-safe put into the asyncio queue.
+                    asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(q.put(chunk), loop).result()
+        except BaseException as e:
+            asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(q.put(e), loop).result()
+        finally:
+            asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(q.put(_SENTINEL), loop).result()
+
+    t = threading.Thread(target=_producer, daemon=True, name="fish-tts-producer")
+    t.start()
+
+    while True:
+        item = await q.get()
+        if item is _SENTINEL:
+            break
+        if isinstance(item, BaseException):
+            raise item
+        yield item
+        # Yield control so ASGI can actually flush the bytes to the socket now.
+        await asyncio.sleep(0)


 async def buffer_to_async_generator(buffer):

Why: the old code was `for chunk in inference(...)` (sync) *inside* an `async
def`. The sync loop blocks the event loop for the full duration of LLAMA
generation, so even the WAV header sitting in the generator can't reach the
socket until generate() returns. The new version pushes the sync generator
into a worker thread and bridges via asyncio.Queue, with an `asyncio.sleep(0)`
after each yield so uvicorn gets a cooperative point to write to the socket.

PATCH B — src/tools/server/inference.py
---------------------------------------
--- a/src/tools/server/inference.py
+++ b/src/tools/server/inference.py
@@ -19,7 +19,15 @@
         match result.code:
             case "header":
                 if isinstance(result.audio, tuple):
-                    yield result.audio[1]
+                    hdr = result.audio[1]
+                    # Convert numpy ndarray (dtype |S44) or similar into plain bytes
+                    # so the ASGI streaming layer flushes it to the socket immediately.
+                    if isinstance(hdr, bytes):
+                        yield hdr
+                    elif hasattr(hdr, "tobytes"):
+                        yield hdr.tobytes()
+                    else:
+                        yield bytes(hdr)

             case "error":
                 raise HTTPException(
@@ -34,8 +42,9 @@

             case "final":
                 count += 1
-                if isinstance(result.audio, tuple):
-                    yield result.audio[1]
+                # Streaming already emitted all segments; the "final" tuple is the
+                # concatenated numpy float32 array (non-streaming path). Converting
+                # it to int16 bytes here would double-emit audio, so skip.
                 return None  # Stop the generator

     if count == 0:

Why:
- The "header" case wrapped the 44-byte WAV header in `np.array(bytes_obj)`,
  producing a 0-d ndarray of dtype `|S44`. The downstream async bridge filtered
  with `isinstance(chunk, bytes)`, which silently discards ndarrays. Net effect:
  the WAV header was never sent, so a browser / ffmpeg client only saw audio
  once the first segment emission drove the actual bytes out. `hdr.tobytes()`
  fixes that by unwrapping to a real `bytes` object.
- The "final" case yielded the *whole* concatenated float32 numpy buffer as raw
  bytes, which is nonsense for a 16-bit-PCM WAV stream (4x sized and wrong
  dtype). In streaming mode all segments were already emitted, so "final" is
  redundant. In non-streaming mode views.py only pulls the first yield via
  next(), so the final case was never reached there. Either way: dropping it
  removes a trailing-garbage chunk without changing correct behavior.

